        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Mahjong (Japanese Riichi)',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Japanese Mahjong, or Riichi Mahjong, is the most competitively evolved form of the Chinese classic. It is characterized by the 'Riichi' mechanicâ€”a declaration of being one card away from winningâ€”and the 'Dora' system of bonus point cards. It is a highly defensive and strategic game where 'not losing' (avoiding dealing into an opponent's hand) is as important as winning.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Mahjong was introduced to Japan in the early 1900s. After WWII, the rules were standardized into the 'Riichi' style to facilitate national tournaments.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players. Wind rotation (East/South).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "136 tiles: 3 suits (1-9), Winds, Dragons. One 'Dora' indicator is turned over in the dead wall.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "136 tiles: 3 suits (1-9), Winds, Dragons. One 'Dora' indicator is turned over in the dead wall.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player starts with 13 tiles; dealer with 14.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Hanchan (Half game): Standard format: East and South rounds; game ends after South round. Used in most tournaments and online play.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Tonpuusen (East-only): Only East round is played; faster format used in some casual and league settings.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Aka Dora (Red fives): One or more red 5 tiles per suit count as Dora and add bonus value when in hand or as Dora indicator.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
